  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10040v1</id>
    <title>!MSA at BAREC Shared Task 2025: Ensembling Arabic Transformers for Readability Assessment</title>
    <updated>2025-09-12T08:08:45Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10040v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.10040v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>We present MSAs winning system for the BAREC 2025 Shared Task on fine-grained Arabic readability assessment, achieving first place in six of six tracks. Our approach is a confidence-weighted ensemble of four complementary transformer models (AraBERTv2, AraELECTRA, MARBERT, and CAMeLBERT) each fine-tuned with distinct loss functions to capture diverse readability signals. To tackle severe class imbalance and data scarcity, we applied weighted training, advanced preprocessing, SAMER corpus relabeling with our strongest model, and synthetic data generation via Gemini 2.5 Flash, adding about 10,000 rare-level samples. A targeted post-processing step corrected prediction distribution skew, delivering a 6.3 percent Quadratic Weighted Kappa (QWK) gain. Our system reached 87.5 percent QWK at the sentence level and 87.4 percent at the document level, demonstrating the power of model and loss diversity, confidence-informed fusion, and intelligent augmentation for robust Arabic readability prediction.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2025-09-12T08:08:45Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>10 Pages , 8 figures , ArabicNLP 2025 , Co-located with EMNLP 2025</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Mohamed Basem</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Mohamed Younes</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Seif Ahmed</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Abdelrahman Moustafa</name>
